    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. so for the live load part, what's the standard psf (pounds per square foot) i should be aiming for with a residential deck in bc? and if i'm worried about the beams sagging over time due to the weight, what are the typical deflection limits i should look at for the cedar beams?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The standard live load for residential decks in British Columbia (BC) is often set at 40 pounds per square foot (psf), although this can vary based on local building codes and the intended use of the deck. When planning your deck to prevent sagging or deflection over time, softwoods like western red cedar usually have a suitable tensile strength for most residential projects. However, you may want to check the International Residential Code (IRC) or local Vancouver codes for their receptiveness to deflection. Typically, the deflection limit for wood floor structures is L/360, where L is the beam length. So for a 12 feet long beam, the maximum allowable deflection would be 1/3 inch before considering necessary adjustments.",
